## `main.rs` and the health probe

```rust
if let Some(code) = probe_exit_code(PORT).await {
    std::process::exit(code);
}

let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind(("0.0.0.0", PORT)).await?;
serve(listener, redis_url(), twin_url(), max_socket_lifetime(), shutdown_signal()).await?;
```

`socket --health` is the container's health check. The image has no shell and no `curl`, so the binary **probes itself**: it calls its own `http://127.0.0.1:3000/health` with a 3 s timeout and exits `0` on any `2xx`, else `1`. Started without the flag, it just serves.

```rust
async fn shutdown_signal() {
    let mut terminate = signal(SignalKind::terminate()).expect("SIGTERM handler is installable");

    tokio::select! {
        _ = tokio::signal::ctrl_c() => {}
        _ = terminate.recv() => {}
    }

    tokio::spawn(async {
        tokio::time::sleep(SHUTDOWN_GRACE).await;
        std::process::exit(1);
    });
}
```

Wait for `Ctrl+C` or `SIGTERM`. Then start a 10 second timer in the background that kills the process with exit code `1`. So open sockets get 10 seconds to close politely, and then the process leaves anyway. (Sockets do not close on their own during a graceful shutdown, which is why the timer is needed.)

---

## Routes and events

Every event, room and payload: [Socket API](../api/asyncapi.html?spec=socket.yaml) (AsyncAPI 3, since Socket.IO pushes events instead of answering requests).

| Kind | Name | Who | What it does |
|---|---|---|---|
| HTTP | `GET /health` | probe, `socket --health` | `200` |
| HTTP | `GET /metrics` | Prometheus | Text format |
| Socket.IO | handshake on `/socket.io/` | `client` | Checks claims, joins the domain rooms |
| Client emit | `subscribe_building` (building id), with ack | `client` | Checks ownership, joins `building:{id}` |
| Client emit | `unsubscribe_building` (building id) | `client` | Leaves `building:{id}` |
| Server emit | `telemetry` | to `building:{id}` | One tick |
| Server emit | `devices` | to `building:{id}` | Device dots of the building |
| Server emit | `notification` | to `domain:{name}` | One notification |

The edge route `/socket.io/*` requires the gateway check, the same as every other gated route.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `REDIS_URL` | none, required | Subscriptions |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://digital-twin:3000` | Ownership lookup |
| `FRONTEND_URL` | `http://localhost:5173` | The one CORS origin allowed |
| `SOCKET_MAX_LIFETIME_SECS` | 900 | The base of the socket lifetime |

The port is fixed at 3000 in code.

---

## Metrics

| Name | Type | Counts |
|---|---|---|
| `telemetry_relayed_total`, `devices_relayed_total`, `notifications_relayed_total` | counter | Messages sent to a room |
| `relay_payload_bytes_total{channel}` | counter | Bytes sent per kind |
| `relay_messages_skipped_total{channel}` | counter | Messages skipped as bad |
| `socket_connections_rejected_total` | counter | Handshakes refused |
| `socket_subscriptions_rejected_total{reason}` | counter | `forbidden`, `lookup_failed` |
| `socket_sessions_expired_total` | counter | Sockets closed by expiry |
| `socket_connected_clients` | gauge | Sockets open now |

Every series exists at 0 before any traffic, so a graph shows 0 and not "no data".

---

## Tests

```bash
just test socket               # unit, no Redis
just test socket-integration   # tests/*.rs against a throwaway Redis
```

| Where | What it checks |
|---|---|
| `src/core/*.rs` | Claims decoding. Domain overlap. Room names. Ack shapes. The lifetime stays within 787.5 to 900 s for base 900 and is stable per id. Relay plans for every case of `schemas/fixtures/notification.json` |
| `src/shell/twin.rs` | The lookup URL, `..` escaped, colons kept. The cache is read back, an empty answer is cached. `503` is retried once, `404` is not. 5 concurrent lookups make 1 request |
| `tests/relay_redis.rs` | Real Redis and a stub `digital-twin`: telemetry and devices reach only that building's subscribers. A scoped notification reaches only its domain, an unscoped one nobody. A refused subscription gets its reason. Unsubscribe stops the feed. No claims, no connection. A bad message does not stop the relay. The relay comes back after Redis drops it. A socket is closed after its lifetime. All metrics at 0 before traffic |
| `tests/architecture.rs` | The layering rules above |

The core tests are the cheap ones: they call a function with a string and compare the result. No Redis, no sockets, no timers. That is the reward for keeping the core free of input and output.

---

## Gaps

- **One copy only.** `k8s/deployments/socket.yml` runs `replicas: 1`, and rooms live in process memory. A restart closes every socket. Each browser reconnects, subscribes again (`onReconnect` in `stores/commons/sensorData.ts`) and refetches what it missed. Every copy reads every Redis message, so more copies would relay correctly, but nothing tests that setup.
- **Ownership is cached 60 s.** A building moved out of a domain stays readable for up to 60 s by sockets that ask in that window. An already joined socket stays in the room until it leaves or expires.
- **A domain room is fixed at connect.** A new membership reaches the socket only after it reconnects.
- **The ownership cache and the gates are never emptied.** Each building id ever asked about keeps an entry and a lock for the life of the process. A browser can send any text as a building id, so a client that asks for many made-up ids makes both maps grow.
- **Messages that are not text are dropped without a trace.** `consume` skips them with no log and no counter.
- **The browser never reads the subscription answer.** `stores/commons/sensorData.ts` and `composables/commons/useSensorData.ts` emit `subscribe_building` with no acknowledgement callback, so a `forbidden` or `lookup_failed` refusal is lost and the building just stays silent.
